WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:10.370
Research sometimes proves, with data, what we more or less already know (e.g. exercise is good for you, and polluted air isn’t).
研究有时会用数据证明我们多少已经知道的事情（例如，锻炼对身体有益，而污染的空气则对身体无益）。

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:18.100
Still, sometimes our assumptions are incorrect, and scientific findings surprise researchers, along with the rest of us.
不过，有时我们的假设并不正确，科学发现会让研究人员以及我们其他人都感到意外。

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:25.230
A recent example is the phenomenon of disappearing lakes in parts of the frozen treeless Arctic region.
最近的一个例子是，在冰冻、无树的北极地区，部分区域出现了湖泊消失的现象。

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:29.490
You might think these lakes would be expanding, not disappearing.
你可能会以为这些湖泊会不断扩大，而不是消失。

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:39.830
As climate change warms the region — melting surface snow and ice as well as the permafrost — there should be more surface water, forming larger lakes and even new ones.
随着气候变化使该地区变暖——融化地表的冰雪以及永冻层——地表水应该会增多，形成更大的湖泊，甚至形成新的湖泊。

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:46.930
Recently, however, scientists have observed not just shrinking lakes but lakes that have completely gone away.
然而，最近科学家们观察到的不仅有正在缩小的湖泊，还有已经完全消失的湖泊。

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:56.110
Many scientists shared the commonsense expectation that as permafrost melted, lakes would at first expand with meltwater flowing into them.
许多科学家都持有一种常识性的预期：随着永冻层融化，湖泊起初会因融水流入而扩大。

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:06.680
Researchers also predicted that eventually progressive warming during the 21st and 22nd centuries would dry out the Arctic, and lakes would begin to shrink.
研究人员还预测，21世纪和22世纪逐渐加剧的变暖最终会使北极变得干燥，湖泊也会开始缩小。

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:11.930
But now it looks as if Arctic lakes are disappearing a century sooner than predicted.
但现在看来，北极湖泊消失的时间比预测的早了一个世纪。

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:19.140
A case in point is that some large, age-old lakes shrank dramatically in what appears to have been a matter of months.
一个典型的例子是，一些古老的大型湖泊在似乎只有短短几个月的时间里急剧缩小。

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:23.520
Scientists have labeled this phenomenon “disastrous lake loss”.
科学家们将这一现象称为“灾难性湖泊消失”。

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.270
What caused this?
是什么导致了这种情况？

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:33.200
The frozen solid ground actually contains bits of rock, mineral and organic matter, leaving spaces in between.
冻得坚硬的地面实际上含有岩石、矿物和有机物的碎块，碎块之间留有空隙。

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:43.022
Because these spaces are filled with solid ice, liquid water cannot readily get inside; when the permafrost melts, however, more water can get through.
由于这些空隙里填满了固态冰，液态水不易渗入；然而，永冻层融化时，更多的水就能渗透进去。

00:01:43.822 --> 00:01:52.362
In one case, soon after the permafrost beneath them melted, around 192 lakes in northwestern Alaska disappeared from sight.
有一个例子是，阿拉斯加西北部约192个湖泊在湖底的永冻层融化后不久就消失不见了。

00:01:53.162 --> 00:01:54.742
Why did scientists miss this?
科学家们为什么没能预见到这一点？

00:01:55.542 --> 00:02:02.952
One probable reason is that most climate models assume that the melting of permafrost is driven only by warming air.
一个可能的原因是，大多数气候模型都假定，永冻层融化仅仅是空气变暖造成的。

00:02:03.752 --> 00:02:12.732
New evidence suggests, however, that rainfall — particularly increasing autumn rain — is now contributing significantly to permafrost loss.
然而，新的证据表明，降雨——尤其是日益增多的秋雨——目前正在大大加剧永冻层的消失。

00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:15.592
The rainfall carries heat into the ground.
雨水将热量带入地下。

00:02:16.392 --> 00:02:19.672
Yet none of the existing models includes such processes.
然而，现有的模型都没有把这样的过程考虑在内。

00:02:20.472 --> 00:02:28.782
That is a good example of why — no matter how complex our models are or may one day be — we need direct observation of the natural world.
这很好地说明了为什么无论我们的模型现在有多复杂，或者将来有一天可能变得多复杂，我们都需要对自然界进行直接观测。
